#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85c8b7c1-d5f9-5e43-ad17-d7531cf574d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094641} {:answer/id #uuid "a1823c83-d9f9-50c7-a159-e7196795cf19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094642} {:answer/id #uuid "c4232482-cc70-53b0-815e-52de28b68e5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094643} {:answer/id #uuid "1516d8b8-ab23-5912-876f-1252a490d6f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094644} {:answer/id #uuid "15896281-698b-5df2-bf0f-91f10b06124c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1823c83-d9f9-50c7-a159-e7196795cf19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094642} :answer-field/id #uuid "66a4a373-312f-5147-8037-485a5d2512c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f14aa2fa-d615-53a6-833b-93cabfaae0ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-14248" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/cac89f66c8c1e5e0ac6a9857cb7db48d3ca6edc2a83fb2ba3a63b0809cfe6ab0.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(5,-4)$ and passes through the point $(2,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(5,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}=4p(y+4).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,5),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-5)^{2} & =y+4 \\\\ 25 & =y+4 \\\\ y & =25-4 \\\\ y & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $21.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e182f71-db24-5c29-9c8b-2fc4b5ab5dd8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Y-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67eeaded-d494-5d1a-960d-f93269f0bed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67eeaded-d494-5d1a-960d-f93269f0bed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276655} :answer-field/id #uuid "d57ef979-b2c9-58f3-af3d-2c9605e2a13f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "feea1d8a-6328-5eb2-9e54-6502ed361d20" :question/math-academy-id "q-251796" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/cec5eacc688d3c3a619aeb6ede2b1c9f9290b8045699259d4c1d933ecefd1835.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(4,-6)$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(4,-6)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4p(y+6).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,3),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2-4)^{2} & =4p(3+6) \\\\ 36 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =4(y+6) \\\\ 16 & =4(y+6) \\\\ 4 & =y+6 \\\\ y & =4-6 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b67ce46-4a76-5e53-baf2-737f4bbdf1d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276658} {:answer/id #uuid "83021b32-a7d8-52b3-a794-f05e6aecda9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $-\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276659} {:answer/id #uuid "e45e5e77-787d-541a-b6e1-0d4af09eb422" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\frac{13}{3}$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276660} {:answer/id #uuid "056dcd5a-4083-5525-a6e6-63cdaa7d363b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\sqrt{6}-1$ and $-\\sqrt{6}+1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276661} {:answer/id #uuid "cb012d8a-9de1-5b74-911d-afed583d3f2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-5$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b67ce46-4a76-5e53-baf2-737f4bbdf1d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ and $-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276658} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a7ad1a6-e720-513d-b510-c1eb2349eae8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0efcadc6-2824-5745-889c-95d91d10c622" :question/math-academy-id "q-73831" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/0a9d728a5927f380bbc88445c6a409974d94050040f23000cce50f70de436917.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-3,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-6,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-3,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-6,8),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=8$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6+3)^{2} & =4p(8+1) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =(0+1) \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $-4$ and $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e34980b-eda0-5d41-a647-b83acca98df5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276665} {:answer/id #uuid "8bb0407b-d482-565f-9d13-63529eadf4af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276666} {:answer/id #uuid "81cdc982-59e6-561e-bf1e-76eff41e1f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276667} {:answer/id #uuid "444c5e7d-d63d-5c26-9878-c76dd8db52c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276668} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa01371-cd88-5d1b-90d2-b9ee19dda065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffa01371-cd88-5d1b-90d2-b9ee19dda065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276669} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb3fc613-d832-5aed-aabc-383ce99aedc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f07bf12-0aed-5e39-ac42-865205552e47" :question/math-academy-id "q-73833" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/9a7f256ae77447f4b426612a3e136ccb60ebd172a6eb472c9fa9948e324b3085.png)\n\nWhat is the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-8,-3)$ and passes through the point $(-2,-6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-8,-3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+3)^{2}=4p(x+8).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,-6),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=-6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6+3)^{2} & =4p(-2+8) \\\\ 9 & =4p(6) \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}(x+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}(x+8) \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x+8) \\\\ \\frac{18}{3} & =x+8 \\\\ x & =6-8 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c990d69-54da-5541-948f-0d5eff96feb5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the X-Intercepts of a Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb823ae9-bd88-55b8-a6f4-d9765841e219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276672} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5a355f-f347-5ffa-ba32-286317332e5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{3}{2}$ and $-\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276673} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8dd277-0a70-5f39-bfad-c90f2f0b3e5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{2}$ and $-\\frac{7}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276674} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e13235-fdc2-5c05-b14d-ad763fc6ee37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276675} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b7d6bf-42e7-57d4-a115-9afe9842b17b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb823ae9-bd88-55b8-a6f4-d9765841e219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ and $-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276672} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bfe0323-745f-532d-bd98-762c74aab9ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "248b559b-2f7e-5113-8ca0-3e8f78ffa304" :question/math-academy-id "q-79252" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13973094/assets/aa95f5a4629b2f97943db55125f91d76de962534be820fe70112132e5fddaaec.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-1,-4)$ and passes through the point $(2,5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-1,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,5),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+1)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =0+4 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $1$ and $-3.$"}]}
